WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:05.350
Imagine an earth-like planet far larger than
our own.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:10.680
NASA has actually discovered numerous exoplanets,
dubbed ‘super-earths,’ which are over

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.360
twice the size of our tiny home-world.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.190
Most of these super-earths can’t support
life, but it’s interesting to imagine what

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:22.609
sorts of organisms we’d discover on a super-sized
version of a habitable world...

00:00:22.609 --> 00:00:28.259
Phtanum B is a speculative project created
by the brilliant Stevemobcannon, who imagines

00:00:28.259 --> 00:00:32.310
just such a planet, and the incredible alien
life that might evolve on it.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:36.610
And as usual, I have links where you can follow
and support his work down below.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:41.590
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
be voyaging to the extraordinary alien world

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:47.980
of Phtanum B. The planet gets its name from
the Greek root wood ‘phtan,’ which translates

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:53.140
as ‘To arrive first’ — a reference to
the idea that Phtanum B is the first planet

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.900
with alien life that humanity arrives on in
this fictional narrative.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:02.740
At almost twice the size of Earth with a higher
surface gravity and thicker atmosphere, conditions

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:06.109
on Phtanum B are quite different from on our
own planet.

00:01:06.109 --> 00:01:12.049
And the life here is downright awe-inspiring,
reaching colossal sizes despite the higher

00:01:12.049 --> 00:01:15.270
gravity… but we’ll come back to that later
in the video.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:19.640
For now, let’s discuss how humanity first
reached these alien shores….

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:26.179
I wish I could tell you that in this timeline,
humans grew into a grand interstellar civilization,

00:01:26.179 --> 00:01:31.470
but the truth is the author imagines humanity
drove themselves to extinction before our

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.039
story begins.

00:01:33.039 --> 00:01:38.700
Their successors are mysterious genetically-modified
superhumans known as the Deum, who wish to

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.369
give their homo-sapiens cousins a second chance.

00:01:42.369 --> 00:01:49.679
Traveling to Phtanum B, the powerful Deum
placed a reborn colony of humans on this habitable

00:01:49.679 --> 00:01:51.959
world — and watched their progress from
afar.

00:01:51.959 --> 00:01:57.600
But what the last of humanity will find on
this planet is an ecosystem much, much different

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.029
from Earth’s…

00:01:59.029 --> 00:02:05.020
Beginning with flora, plants — like everything
on Phtanum B, reach gargantuan sizes.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:10.670
Here on the southern supercontinent, a lush
world of red-tinted vegetation flourishes.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:15.700
The sheer amount of consumable biomass in
this environment far exceeds that of any on

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:21.530
Earth, allowing the organisms that feed on
this flora to reach equally staggering sizes.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:26.860
One of the first of these animals that humans
encounter are the Giant Ptyonocodite — relatively

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:32.400
common herbivores with iridescent green skin
due to algae that grows on their body and

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.340
symbiotically supplies them with extra energy.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:40.379
Giant Ptyonocodite also gain energy by consuming
plant matter in the surrounding environment

00:02:40.379 --> 00:02:44.920
— which they rip from the ground with their
flexible arm-like jaws.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:49.480
Strange as this might seem, Ptyonocodite anatomy
is a good ‘average’ example of a body

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:56.829
plan on Phtanum B — so let’s take a closer
look at the inner workings of these organisms.

00:02:56.829 --> 00:03:01.349
The skeletal system of Ptyonocodites and many
of the other massive lifeforms we’ll be

00:03:01.349 --> 00:03:06.590
exploring is made of pyrite — able to support
far greater weight than the hydroxyapatite

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:08.500
bones of earthly animals.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:13.800
Likewise, their muscle system is more powerful
than an earth organism’s, with core muscles

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:18.840
made of more long-chained myosin fibers for
basic movements, and a dedicated secondary

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.939
system of muscles for precise movement.

00:03:21.939 --> 00:03:26.780
Instead of a single brain, a decentralized
neural system sends commands to these muscles

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:30.890
— not unlike the neural systems of some
types of starfish and coral.

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:36.239
Finally, you’ll notice that instead of eyes,
most lifeforms on Phtanum B have ridges lined

00:03:36.239 --> 00:03:38.890
with infrared light receptors on their heads.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:43.599
Most organisms also lack traditional mouths,
instead having a mouth-like hole in between

00:03:43.599 --> 00:03:47.170
two tentacle-like ‘arm-jaws’ that bring
food into the opening.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:53.970
But keep in mind Ptyonocodites are just a
baseline, and life on Phtanum B gets far more

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:54.970
interesting…

00:03:54.970 --> 00:04:00.659
A distant relative of the Ptyonocodites are
the plains-dwelling Bronze Synylopes — lifeforms

00:04:00.659 --> 00:04:05.390
that use their strange, shovel-like lower
arm-jaw to dig for food.

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:10.180
These organisms move in great herds of up
to thirty individuals — a survival strategy

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:13.890
similar to the herding instincts of many animals
here on Earth.

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:19.720
At 10 feet, or 3.2 meters in length, Bronze
Synylopes could be considered megafauna here

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:23.900
on earth… but on Phtanum B, they’re actually
on the small side.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:29.210
This has led some human explorers to attempt
domesticating them, with mixed success.

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:34.180
Along the volcanic shores of Phtanum B’s
coastal regions, a hunter is relentlessly

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:36.210
pursuing prey.

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:41.950
This is a Black Curvoglavid, a multi-limbed
predator whose arm jaws have hardened into

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:46.389
curved structures lined with pseudo-teeth
used to ensnare its next meal.

00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:51.500
In this regard, the spiny jaws of a Black
Curvoglavid are analogous to the mandibles

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:53.530
of some earth insects.

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:58.479
These endurance hunters might be deadly to
humans caught outside the walls of their colony,

00:04:58.479 --> 00:05:02.370
but they’re actually just midsized predators
on this world — which, when compared to

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:06.860
midsized Earth predators, puts into perspective
how much bigger and scarier life can grow

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:12.670
on Phtanum B. Less common are Great Frigatepedes
— rarely-encountered lifeforms that almost

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:16.030
resemble extremely deadly living trains.

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:19.990
Their multi-limbed body structure in some
ways calls to mind the appearance of Earth

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:23.300
insects like centipedes or caterpillars.

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:28.460
Curiously, Great Frigatepedes hunt prey by
ramming into them at high speeds, with their

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:32.110
bony front body protrusion acting as a battering
ram.

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:37.250
After a successful use of this strange hunting
technique, the creature’s hooked jaw-arms

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:40.590
unfold from hidden pouches and tear apart
the prey.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:46.259
And if that’s not intimidating enough, Great
Frigatepedes are true goliaths, reaching almost

00:05:46.259 --> 00:05:52.840
64 feet, or 19.5 meters in length… although
some of their distant relatives grow even

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:53.840
larger.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:58.620
But before we meet the relatives of the Frigatepedes,
there’s another fascinating lifeform lurking

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:00.199
in the snowy mountains.

00:06:00.199 --> 00:06:06.039
Here, far from the red plants of the lowlands,
you can see the remains of its latest kill…

00:06:06.039 --> 00:06:08.600
meaning our culprit is close by.

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:14.060
This is the Beartraphugger, a mountainous
hunter that possesses a shaggy coat of silvery

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:19.460
fibers to protect it from the cold, not unlike
the fur of a polar bear on our own planet.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:24.889
Beartraphugger ancestry isn’t fully understood,
but the bizarre biting apparatus at the front

00:06:24.889 --> 00:06:29.330
of their head is an effective, knife-like
tool when it comes to hunting prey.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:33.190
But far stranger hunters exist on Phtanum
B…

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:37.880
Returning to the lowlands, even the trees
on Phtanum B can be deadly — although the

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:43.250
Sathrical Corpsetree isn’t a tree in the
traditional sense but a Xenicozoophyte — an

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:49.039
organism that can gain sustenance from both
the sun and other sources: which in the Corpsetree’s

00:06:49.039 --> 00:06:51.240
case, includes living prey.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:56.599
Blending into the red vegetation, these odd
predators strike down animals that wander

00:06:56.599 --> 00:06:59.650
too close with the large claw at the end of
their arm.

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:04.479
The speared prey is then inserted into a mouth-like
hole near the base of the arm.

00:07:04.479 --> 00:07:06.000
Pretty terrifying.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:11.759
Another Xenicozoophyte is the Blitz Spire,
a lifeform so bizarre and unearthly it’s

00:07:11.759 --> 00:07:13.360
hard to grasp at first.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:18.800
The single largest immobile organism on Phtanum
B, the Blitz Spire can reach a staggering

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:27.950
1,600 feet, or 495 meters tall, and grow indefinitely
throughout their lifecycle until they collapse

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:29.970
under their weight and mass.

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:35.699
The tip of the Blitz Spire has evolved, incredibly,
to attract lightning strikes, the electric

00:07:35.699 --> 00:07:40.300
currents of which can then be re-released
to stun prey that gets close to its underground

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:41.759
feeding tendrils.

00:07:41.759 --> 00:07:46.240
These living skyscrapers are truly a lifeform
unlike any other.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:52.060
The largest mobile predator in both modern
and prehistoric times, however, is the Giant

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:55.910
Lactismid — which has reached a scale most
carnivores could only dream of.

00:07:55.910 --> 00:08:02.750
A larger relative of the Frigatepedes, Giant
Lactismids can reach 102 feet, or 31.1 meters

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:03.780
in length.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:09.199
The incredible bulk of this creature is supported
by countless legs: including larger, weight-bearing

00:08:09.199 --> 00:08:14.370
outer limbs, and pairs of thinner, hooked
internal limbs that can aid in hunting prey.

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:20.190
Giant Lactismids also have alarmingly well-developed
neural-systems, which makes them aware enough

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:24.449
to avoid most human settlements… although
on rare occasions, they’ve been known to

00:08:24.449 --> 00:08:26.419
hunt humans for sport.

00:08:26.419 --> 00:08:31.509
But even for a Giant Lactismid, not all lifeforms
are easy to take down.

00:08:31.509 --> 00:08:36.909
Take the gargantuan Castlewalkers, a group
that, as their name implies, are impenetrable

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:42.820
living fortresses — and some of the heaviest
terrestrial grazers on Phtanum B. Occupying

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:47.730
a niche not entirely unlike an elephant on
our planet, Castlewalkers vacuum up plant

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:53.240
matter with a long, jaw-arm appendage covered
in quills to scare off any predators.

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:57.160
These creatures are also quite intelligent,
and are able to communicate with other members

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:01.850
of their species with morse code-like ground
vibrations that they can perceive through

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:02.850
their feet.

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:07.459
As you can see by this size comparison to
other Phtanum B lifeforms — plus a T-rex

00:09:07.459 --> 00:09:11.890
for good measure — Castlewalkers are true
titans, reaching lengths of over one hundred

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:14.440
and ten feet, or 34 meters.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:20.300
But even Castlewalkers are small in comparison
to the final lifeform on our list…

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:26.470
The immense Red Windwhales are among the most
legendary and awe-inspiring lifeforms of Phtanum

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:31.640
B. Gliding through the open skies, Windwhales
are mainly made up of air-filled cavities

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:39.260
that allow them to stay aloft despite reaching
lengths of over 425 feet, or 130 meters — and

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:42.090
possessing wingspans nearly twice that length.

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:46.240
Aided by the dense atmosphere, Windwhales
function almost like living paper planes,

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:50.850
and possess retractable legs that work like
grappling hooks to anchor them to the ground

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:52.320
in turbulent weather.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:57.430
When these tethers fail, however, and a Windwhale
passes away, they create something called

00:09:57.430 --> 00:10:02.839
a ‘Whalefall’ — an event where organisms
from all across Phatnum B come to feast on

00:10:02.839 --> 00:10:03.839
the fallen titan.

00:10:03.839 --> 00:10:08.440
For the saga of Phatnum B is a story of life
and death.

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:11.000
And now, humans are a part of this story…

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:15.540
At this point in the narrative’s development,
whether or not humans will be able to survive

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:18.160
long term in their new environment is unknown.

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:23.620
Phtanum B is an ongoing project, so who knows
what else might happens—and what else might

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:26.010
be lurking on this alien world…

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:27.750
Thanks for watching.

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:32.290
Please consider supporting the author using
the links below — if you enjoyed this entry,

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:37.550
please like, subscribe, and hit the notification
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:46.890
See you in the next video.

